{"name":"Blue Ballerina","hex":"#b4c7db","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"ballerina","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ballet dancer \n A female ballet dancer.\nEllipsis of prima ballerina (“the star female ballet performer in the company”).","A female ballet dancer.\nEllipsis of prima ballerina (“the star female ballet performer in the company”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ballerina"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ballet dancer","blocks":[{"text":"A ballet dancer is a person who practices the art of classical ballet. Both women and men can practice ballet. They rely on years of extensive training and proper technique to become a part of a professional ballet company. Ballet dancers are at a high risk of injury due to the demanding technique of ballet dancing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Corsaire_-Le_Jardin_Anime_-Pierina_Legnani_-Olga_Preobrajenska_-1899.jpg/330px-Corsaire_-Le_Jardin_Anime_-Pierina_Legnani_-Olga_Preobrajenska_-1899.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballet_dancer","wikidata":"Q805221"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Monochrome_Ballet_Feet_%28Unsplash%29.jpg/500px-Monochrome_Ballet_Feet_%28Unsplash%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Monochrome_Ballet_Feet_%28Unsplash%29.jpg/1280px-Monochrome_Ballet_Feet_%28Unsplash%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Monochrome_Ballet_Feet_%28Unsplash%29.jpg/500px-Monochrome_Ballet_Feet_%28Unsplash%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Many ballet dancers end up with injured feet due to the size of the shoes or the use of their feet in choreography","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMonochrome_Ballet_Feet_(Unsplash).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Ballet-Ballerina-1843.jpg/500px-Ballet-Ballerina-1843.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Ballet-Ballerina-1843.jpg/1280px-Ballet-Ballerina-1843.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Ballet-Ballerina-1843.jpg/500px-Ballet-Ballerina-1843.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut